<worksheet xml:id="worksheet-assignment-6" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Assignment 6</title>

<introduction>
<p>
These problems intersect lines with planes and measure distances between
them, match equations to pictures, identify a quadric surface from its
equation by slicing it with the coordinate planes and sketch it, and identify
conic sections from their equations. If you would like to review the material
first, see
<xref ref="sec-skel-geom-lines-planes" text="title"/>,
<xref ref="sec-skel-geom-quadric-surfaces" text="title"/> and
<xref ref="sec-skel-geom-conics" text="title"/>.
</p>
</introduction>

<exercise xml:id="rf-4">
<statement>
<p>
Find the vector equation of the line of intersection of the following
planes:
<md>
<mrow>2x + y - z \amp = 6,</mrow>
<mrow>x - y + 3z \amp = 4.</mrow>
</md>
</p>
</statement>
<!-- SOLUTION-LOCKED assignment="6" due="2026-10-28"
<solution>
<p>
The normal vectors are <m>\vec n_1 = \langle 2, 1, -1\rangle</m> and
<m>\vec n_2 = \langle 1, -1, 3\rangle</m>, so the direction vector of
the line of intersection is
<md>
\mathbf v = \vec n_1 \times \vec n_2 =
\begin{vmatrix}
\mathbf i \amp \mathbf j \amp \mathbf k \\
2 \amp 1 \amp -1 \\
1 \amp -1 \amp 3
\end{vmatrix}
= \langle 2, -7, -3\rangle.
</md>
</p>

<p>
Next we need a point on the line. Setting <m>x = 0</m> in the
equations of the planes gives
<md>
<mrow>y - z \amp = 6</mrow>
<mrow>-y + 3z \amp = 4</mrow>
</md>
Adding the two equations gives <m>2z = 10</m>, so <m>z = 5</m> and
<m>y = 11</m>. Hence the point <m>(0, 11, 5)</m> is on the line, and
the vector equation of the line of intersection is
<md>
\mathbf r(t) = \langle 0, 11, 5\rangle + t\,\langle 2, -7, -3\rangle.
</md>
</p>
</solution>
END SOLUTION-LOCKED -->
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rw21-5">
<statement>
<p>
Find an equation of a plane containing the line
<m>\mathbf r = \langle -4, 5, -1\rangle + t\langle -4, 6, -3\rangle</m>
and parallel to the plane <m>-3x - y + 2z = 0</m>, in which the
coefficient of <m>x</m> is <m>-3</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<!-- SOLUTION-LOCKED assignment="6" due="2026-10-28"
<solution>
<p>
Since the desired plane is parallel to <m>-3x - y + 2z = 0</m>, it has
the same normal vector
<md>
\vec n = \langle -3, -1, 2\rangle = \langle A, B, C\rangle.
</md>
(Note that <m>\vec n \cdot \langle -4, 6, -3\rangle
= 12 - 6 - 6 = 0</m>, so the line is indeed parallel to such a plane.)
When <m>t = 0</m>, the point <m>(-4, 5, -1)</m> is on the plane, so
from the point-normal form,
<md>
<mrow>A(x - x_0) + B(y - y_0) + C(z - z_0) \amp = 0</mrow>
<mrow>\Rightarrow\quad -3(x+4) - (y-5) + 2(z+1) \amp = 0.</mrow>
</md>
</p>
</solution>
END SOLUTION-LOCKED -->
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rs19-5">
<statement>
<p>
Calculate the distance between the following two lines:
<md>
<mrow>L_1: \amp \quad x = 4 - t, \quad y = 3 + 2t, \quad z = -5 + 3t</mrow>
<mrow>L_2: \amp \quad x = 5 - t, \quad y = 3 + 2t, \quad z = -5 + 3t</mrow>
</md>
</p>
</statement>
<!-- SOLUTION-LOCKED assignment="6" due="2026-10-28"
<solution>
<p>
We notice that the two lines are parallel (both have direction vector
<m>\mathbf v = \langle -1, 2, 3\rangle</m>) and therefore we can find
the distance between a point on <m>L_2</m> and the line <m>L_1</m>.
Taking <m>S(5, 3, -5)</m> on <m>L_2</m> and <m>P(4, 3, -5)</m> on
<m>L_1</m>, we get <m>\vec{PS} = \langle 1, 0, 0\rangle</m>, and
<m>\|\mathbf v\| = \sqrt{1 + 4 + 9} = \sqrt{14}</m>.
</p>

<p>
Using the cross product,
<md>
\vec{PS} \times \mathbf v =
\begin{vmatrix}
\mathbf i \amp \mathbf j \amp \mathbf k \\
1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
-1 \amp 2 \amp 3
\end{vmatrix}
= -3\,\mathbf j + 2\,\mathbf k,
\qquad
\left\|\vec{PS} \times \mathbf v\right\| = \sqrt{9 + 4} = \sqrt{13},
</md>
and the distance is
<md>
d = \frac{\left\|\vec{PS} \times \mathbf v\right\|}{\|\mathbf v\|}
= \frac{\sqrt{13}}{\sqrt{14}}.
</md>
</p>
</solution>
END SOLUTION-LOCKED -->
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="pp-17">
<statement>
<p>
Find parametric equations for the line through <m>(2,1,1)</m> which is
normal to the plane containing <m>(0,1,1)</m>, <m>(-1,2,4)</m> and
<m>(3,0,1)</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<!-- SOLUTION-LOCKED assignment="6" due="2026-10-28"
<solution>
<p>
From <xref ref="pp-13"/>, the plane through those three points has
normal vector <m>\vec n = \langle 3, 9, -2\rangle</m>. A line normal
to the plane has <m>\vec n</m> as its direction vector, so through
<m>(2,1,1)</m>,
<md>
x = 2 + 3t, \qquad y = 1 + 9t, \qquad z = 1 - 2t,
\qquad t \in \R.
</md>
</p>
</solution>
END SOLUTION-LOCKED -->
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="m2b-5">
<statement>
<p>
Let <m>L</m> be the line given by
<md>
x = 4+t, \qquad y = -1-2t, \qquad z = 5+t .
</md>
Find the point on <m>L</m> that is closest to <m>(-2,2,-1)</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<!-- SOLUTION-LOCKED assignment="6" due="2026-10-28"
<solution>
<p>
Write <m>Q = (-2,2,-1)</m> and let
<m>P(t) = (4+t,\ -1-2t,\ 5+t)</m> be the general point of <m>L</m>. The
direction vector of the line is
<m>\vec d = \left\langle 1,\ -2,\ 1\right\rangle</m>.
</p>

<p>
The closest point is the foot of the perpendicular from <m>Q</m>, so we
want the value of <m>t</m> for which <m>\vec{QP}</m> is orthogonal to
<m>\vec d</m>. First,
<md>
<mrow>\vec{QP} \amp = \left\langle (4+t)-(-2),\ (-1-2t)-2,\ (5+t)-(-1)
\right\rangle</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \left\langle 6+t,\ -3-2t,\ 6+t \right\rangle .</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<p>
Now impose <m>\vec{QP}\cdot\vec d = 0</m>:
<md>
<mrow>(6+t)(1) + (-3-2t)(-2) + (6+t)(1) \amp = 0</mrow>
<mrow>6 + t + 6 + 4t + 6 + t \amp = 0</mrow>
<mrow>18 + 6t \amp = 0</mrow>
<mrow>t \amp = -3 .</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<p>
Substituting <m>t = -3</m> into the parametric equations gives the
closest point
<md>
P(-3) = \left(4-3,\ -1+6,\ 5-3\right) = (1,\ 5,\ 2) .
</md>
As a check, at <m>t=-3</m> we get
<m>\vec{QP} = \left\langle 3,\ 3,\ 3 \right\rangle</m>, and
<md>
\vec{QP}\cdot\vec d
 = \left\langle 3,3,3\right\rangle\cdot\left\langle 1,-2,1\right\rangle
 = 3 - 6 + 3 = 0 ,
</md>
as required. The distance from <m>Q</m> to the line is
<m>\|\vec{QP}\| = \sqrt{9+9+9} = 3\sqrt3</m>.
</p>
</solution>
END SOLUTION-LOCKED -->
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="dp-constrained-work">
<statement>
<p>
A constant force is
<md>
\mathbf F = \left\langle 2, -1, 3 \right\rangle .
</md>
A particle is constrained to move in directions parallel to the plane
<md>
2x - y + 2z = 0 .
</md>
For a displacement of one unit, the work done by the force is
<m>W = \mathbf F\cdot\mathbf d</m>, where <m>\mathbf d</m> is an
allowed unit direction lying in the plane.
<ol marker="(a)">
<li>
<p>
Find the unit direction in the plane that maximizes the work.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Find the maximum possible work per unit distance.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Find one unit direction in the plane for which the work is zero.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<!-- SOLUTION-LOCKED assignment="6" due="2026-10-28"
<solution>
<p>
Reading the coefficients of the plane, a normal vector is
<md>
\mathbf n = \left\langle 2, -1, 2 \right\rangle ,
\qquad \|\mathbf n\| = \sqrt{4+1+4} = 3 .
</md>
A vector is parallel to the plane exactly when it is orthogonal to
<m>\mathbf n</m>.
</p>

<p>
<term>(a)</term> Decompose the force into a normal piece and a piece
lying in the plane,
<md>
\mathbf F = \operatorname{proj}_{\mathbf n}\mathbf F
          + \mathbf F_{\parallel} .
</md>
The first piece comes from the projection formula. Since
<md>
<mrow>\mathbf F\cdot\mathbf n \amp
= \left\langle 2,-1,3\right\rangle\cdot\left\langle 2,-1,2\right\rangle
= 2(2) + (-1)(-1) + 3(2)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = 4 + 1 + 6 = 11 ,</mrow>
</md>
and <m>\|\mathbf n\|^2 = 9</m>, we get
<md>
\operatorname{proj}_{\mathbf n}\mathbf F
= \frac{11}{9}\left\langle 2,-1,2\right\rangle
= \left\langle \frac{22}{9},\ -\frac{11}{9},\ \frac{22}{9}\right\rangle .
</md>
Subtracting,
<md>
<mrow>\mathbf F_{\parallel} \amp
= \mathbf F - \operatorname{proj}_{\mathbf n}\mathbf F
= \left\langle 2,-1,3\right\rangle
- \left\langle \frac{22}{9},\ -\frac{11}{9},\ \frac{22}{9}\right\rangle</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \left\langle -\frac49,\ \frac29,\ \frac59\right\rangle
= \frac19\left\langle -4,\ 2,\ 5 \right\rangle .</mrow>
</md>
As a check, this really is parallel to the plane:
<m>\left\langle -4,2,5\right\rangle\cdot\left\langle 2,-1,2\right\rangle
= -8 - 2 + 10 = 0</m>.
</p>

<p>
Now let <m>\mathbf d</m> be any allowed unit direction. Because
<m>\operatorname{proj}_{\mathbf n}\mathbf F</m> is orthogonal to every
vector lying in the plane,
<md>
W = \mathbf F\cdot\mathbf d
  = \left(\operatorname{proj}_{\mathbf n}\mathbf F
    + \mathbf F_{\parallel}\right)\cdot\mathbf d
  = \mathbf F_{\parallel}\cdot\mathbf d .
</md>
The normal part of the force is simply wasted: it presses the particle
against the constraint and does no work. Writing
<m>\mathbf F_{\parallel}\cdot\mathbf d
= \|\mathbf F_{\parallel}\|\,\|\mathbf d\|\cos\varphi
= \|\mathbf F_{\parallel}\|\cos\varphi</m>, the largest value occurs when
<m>\cos\varphi = 1</m>, that is when <m>\mathbf d</m> points in the same
direction as <m>\mathbf F_{\parallel}</m>. Since
<md>
\left\|\left\langle -4,2,5\right\rangle\right\| = \sqrt{16+4+25}
= \sqrt{45} = 3\sqrt5 ,
</md>
the maximizing unit vector is
<md>
\mathbf d_{\max}
= \frac{\left\langle -4,\ 2,\ 5\right\rangle}{3\sqrt5} .
</md>
See <xref ref="fig-dp-constrained-work"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-dp-constrained-work">
<caption>
The force <m>\mathbf F</m> splits into a piece
<m>\operatorname{proj}_{\mathbf n}\mathbf F</m> along the normal, which
does no work, and a much shorter piece <m>\mathbf F_{\parallel}</m>
lying in the plane, which is the only part that can do work. The
picture is drawn to scale:
<m>\left\|\operatorname{proj}_{\mathbf n}\mathbf F\right\| = \tfrac{11}{3}</m>
while <m>\left\|\mathbf F_{\parallel}\right\| = \tfrac{\sqrt5}{3}</m>.
</caption>
<image width="85%">
<description>
<p>
A tilted plane through the origin with a normal arrow. A force vector
rises steeply from the origin, almost along the normal; it is resolved
into a long component along the normal and a very short component lying
in the plane.
</p>
</description>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-dp-constrained-work">
<diagram dimensions="(600,310)" margins="16">
<coordinates bbox="(-4.7,-1.6,6.0,4.0)">
<polygon at="dpw-plane"
         points="((-0.70,0.30),(5.30,-1.20),(1.90,1.50),(-4.10,3.00))"
         closed="yes"
         stroke="#0072B2" thickness="2"
         fill="#0072B2" fill-opacity="0.12"/>
<line at="dpw-dash1" p1="(0.701,3.143)" p2="(1.135,3.034)"
      stroke="#7F7F7F" thickness="1" dash="4 3"/>
<line at="dpw-dash2" p1="(0.701,3.143)" p2="(0.166,1.009)"
      stroke="#7F7F7F" thickness="1" dash="4 3"/>
<vector at="dpw-n" v="(0.316,1.261)" tail="(3.3,0.25)"
        stroke="black" thickness="2"/>
<vector at="dpw-proj" v="(0.535,2.134)" tail="(0.6,0.9)"
        stroke="#8B1A4A" thickness="3"/>
<vector at="dpw-force" v="(0.101,2.243)" tail="(0.6,0.9)"
        stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3"/>
<vector at="dpw-par" v="(-0.434,0.109)" tail="(0.6,0.9)"
        stroke="#009E73" thickness="4"/>
<point at="dpw-origin" p="(0.6,0.9)" fill="black"/>
<label at="dpw-originl" anchor="(0.66,0.78)" alignment="se">
<m>O</m>
</label>
<label at="dpw-nl" anchor="(3.68,1.56)" alignment="e">
<m>\mathbf n=\left\langle 2,-1,2\right\rangle</m>
</label>
<label at="dpw-forcel" anchor="(0.62,3.24)" alignment="nw" color="#D55E00">
<m>\mathbf F</m>
</label>
<label at="dpw-projl" anchor="(1.22,3.06)" alignment="ne" color="#8B1A4A">
<m>\operatorname{proj}_{\mathbf n}\mathbf F</m>
</label>
<label at="dpw-parl" anchor="(0.08,0.97)" alignment="w" color="#009E73">
<m>\mathbf F_{\parallel}</m>
</label>
<label at="dpw-planel" anchor="(5.1,-1.32)" alignment="s" color="#0072B2">
<m>2x-y+2z=0</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A blue parallelogram represents the plane two x minus y plus two z equals zero, drawn through the origin O. A black arrow off to the right shows the normal direction n. From O, a long dark red arrow rises steeply as the projection of F onto n, a slightly tilted orange arrow of almost the same length is the force F itself, and a very short green arrow lying in the plane is the component of F parallel to the plane. Dashed grey segments complete the rectangle of the decomposition.">
  <annotation ref="dpw-plane" text="The constraint plane, two x minus y plus two z equals zero, passing through the origin."/>
  <annotation ref="dpw-n" text="A normal vector n to the plane, with components two, negative one, two."/>
  <annotation ref="dpw-force" text="The force F, rising steeply and therefore nearly perpendicular to the plane."/>
  <annotation ref="dpw-proj" text="The projection of F onto the normal, the long component that does no work."/>
  <annotation ref="dpw-par" text="The component of F parallel to the plane, very short compared with the normal component; this is the only part of the force that can do work."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
<term>(b)</term> The maximum work per unit displacement is the length of
the in-plane component,
<md>
<mrow>W_{\max} \amp = \left\|\mathbf F_{\parallel}\right\|
= \left|\frac19\left\langle -4,2,5\right\rangle\right|</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \frac19\left(3\sqrt5\right) = \frac{\sqrt5}{3}
\approx 0.745 .</mrow>
</md>
We can confirm this directly from the original force:
<md>
<mrow>\mathbf F\cdot\mathbf d_{\max} \amp
= \left\langle 2,-1,3\right\rangle\cdot
  \frac{\left\langle -4,2,5\right\rangle}{3\sqrt5}
= \frac{-8-2+15}{3\sqrt5}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \frac{5}{3\sqrt5} = \frac{\sqrt5}{3} .</mrow>
</md>
Compare this with <m>\|\mathbf F\| = \sqrt{14} \approx 3.74</m>: the
constraint costs us about <m>80\%</m> of the force.
</p>

<p>
<term>(c)</term> We want a unit vector
<m>\mathbf d = \left\langle x,y,z\right\rangle</m> satisfying two
conditions at once: it must lie in the plane, and it must do no work.
That is,
<md>
\mathbf d\cdot\mathbf n = 0 \qquad\text{and}\qquad
\mathbf d\cdot\mathbf F = 0 ,
</md>
or explicitly
<md>
<mrow>2x - y + 2z \amp = 0 ,</mrow>
<mrow>2x - y + 3z \amp = 0 .</mrow>
</md>
Subtracting the first equation from the second gives <m>z = 0</m>, and
then <m>2x - y = 0</m>, so <m>y = 2x</m>. Taking <m>x = 1</m> gives the
non-zero vector <m>\left\langle 1,2,0\right\rangle</m>, whose length is
<m>\sqrt{1+4} = \sqrt5</m>. Hence one suitable unit vector is
<md>
\mathbf d_0 = \frac{1}{\sqrt5}\left\langle 1,\ 2,\ 0\right\rangle .
</md>
Indeed <m>\mathbf F\cdot\mathbf d_0
= \tfrac{1}{\sqrt5}(2 - 2 + 0) = 0</m>, and the opposite direction
<m>-\mathbf d_0</m> works equally well. Geometrically, <m>\mathbf d_0</m>
is the direction in the plane perpendicular to
<m>\mathbf F_{\parallel}</m>: moving along it, the particle travels
neither with nor against the usable part of the force.
</p>
</solution>
END SOLUTION-LOCKED -->
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="exercise-quadric-graph-match">
  <title>Matching Quadric Surfaces</title>
  <introduction>
    <image xml:id="image-quadric-match-grid" width="100%">
      <shortdescription>
        Six quadric surfaces labeled A through F: an ellipsoid, a cone
        opening along the y axis, a saddle, a cylinder parallel to the
        y axis, a paraboloid opening along the positive y axis, and two
        bowl-shaped pieces meeting at the origin.
      </shortdescription>
      <latex-image>
        <![CDATA[\scalebox{0.62}{
\begin{tikzpicture}
\definecolor{oiblue}{RGB}{0,114,178}
\pgfplotsset{
  quadric/.style={
    view={115}{20},
    width=5.6cm,
    axis lines=center,
    axis on top,
    axis line style={-latex, black!75, line width=0.5pt},
    xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, zlabel={$z$},
    label style={font=\normalsize},
    ticks=none,
    clip=false,
  },
  surfstyle/.style 2 args={
    surf, shader=faceted,
    colormap={soft#1}{color=(#2!14!white) color=(#2!48!white)},
    faceted color=black!45, line width=0.15pt,
    z buffer=sort,
  },
}
% ---- A: ellipsoid ----
\begin{axis}[quadric, at={(0cm,0cm)}, anchor=north west,
    xmin=-1.6, xmax=1.6, ymin=-1.9, ymax=1.9, zmin=-1.9, zmax=1.9]
  \addplot3[surfstyle={A}{oiblue},
      domain=0:360, samples=29, y domain=0:180, samples y=19]
    ({sin(y)*cos(x)}, {1.3*sin(y)*sin(x)}, {1.6*cos(y)});
\end{axis}
\node[font=\Large\bfseries] at (2.8cm, -5.3cm) {A};
% ---- B: cone along y ----
\begin{axis}[quadric, at={(6cm,0cm)}, anchor=north west,
    xmin=-2.3, xmax=2.3, ymin=-2.4, ymax=2.4, zmin=-1.9, zmax=1.9]
  \addplot3[surfstyle={B}{oiblue},
      domain=0:360, samples=29, y domain=-2:2, samples y=19]
    ({y*cos(x)}, {y}, {0.707*y*sin(x)});
\end{axis}
\node[font=\Large\bfseries] at (8.8cm, -5.3cm) {B};
% ---- C: saddle y = x^2 - z^2 ----
\begin{axis}[quadric, at={(12cm,0cm)}, anchor=north west,
    xmin=-1.7, xmax=1.7, ymin=-2.4, ymax=2.4, zmin=-1.7, zmax=1.7]
  \addplot3[surfstyle={C}{oiblue},
      domain=-1.5:1.5, samples=21, y domain=-1.5:1.5, samples y=19]
    ({x}, {x^2 - y^2}, {y});
\end{axis}
\node[font=\Large\bfseries] at (14.8cm, -5.3cm) {C};
% ---- D: cylinder x^2 + 2z^2 = 1 ----
\begin{axis}[quadric, at={(0cm,-6.4cm)}, anchor=north west,
    xmin=-1.6, xmax=1.6, ymin=-2.4, ymax=2.4, zmin=-1.4, zmax=1.4]
  \addplot3[surfstyle={D}{oiblue},
      domain=0:360, samples=29, y domain=-2:2, samples y=15]
    ({cos(x)}, {y}, {0.707*sin(x)});
\end{axis}
\node[font=\Large\bfseries] at (2.8cm, -11.7cm) {D};
% ---- E: paraboloid y = 2x^2 + z^2 ----
\begin{axis}[quadric, at={(6cm,-6.4cm)}, anchor=north west,
    xmin=-1.4, xmax=1.4, ymin=-0.5, ymax=3.0, zmin=-1.6, zmax=1.6]
  \addplot3[surfstyle={E}{oiblue},
      domain=0:360, samples=29, y domain=0:1.5, samples y=15]
    ({0.707*y*cos(x)}, {y*y}, {y*sin(x)});
\end{axis}
\node[font=\Large\bfseries] at (8.8cm, -11.7cm) {E};
% ---- F: two bowls y = +-(x^2 + z^2) ----
\begin{axis}[quadric, at={(12cm,-6.4cm)}, anchor=north west,
    xmin=-1.6, xmax=1.6, ymin=-2.6, ymax=2.6, zmin=-1.6, zmax=1.6]
  \addplot3[surfstyle={F}{oiblue},
      domain=0:360, samples=29, y domain=0:1.5, samples y=15]
    ({y*cos(x)}, {y*y}, {y*sin(x)});
  \addplot3[surfstyle={F2}{oiblue},
      domain=0:360, samples=29, y domain=0:1.5, samples y=15]
    ({y*cos(x)}, {-y*y}, {y*sin(x)});
\end{axis}
\node[font=\Large\bfseries] at (14.8cm, -11.7cm) {F};
\end{tikzpicture}
}]]>
      </latex-image>
    </image>
  </introduction>
  <webwork>
    <pg-code>
      Context()->strings->add(
        "A" => {}, "B" => {}, "C" => {},
        "D" => {}, "E" => {}, "F" => {},
      );
      $match1 = String("E");
      $match2 = String("B");
      $match3 = String("D");
      $match4 = String("C");
    </pg-code>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Match the equations of the surfaces with the graphs A<ndash/>F shown
        above by entering a letter from A to F in each blank.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>y = 2x^2 + z^2</m> matches graph <var name="$match1" width="4"/>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>y^2 = x^2 + 2z^2</m> matches graph <var name="$match2" width="4"/>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>x^2 + 2z^2 = 1</m> matches graph <var name="$match3" width="4"/>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>y = x^2 - z^2</m> matches graph <var name="$match4" width="4"/>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <!-- SOLUTION-LOCKED assignment="6" due="2026-10-28"
    <solution>
      <p>
        We identify each surface by slicing, just as in this section.  Here
        the special axis is the <m>y</m>-axis, so we slice with the planes
        <m>y = c</m>, <m>x = c</m>, and <m>z = c</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        For <m>y = 2x^2 + z^2</m>, the plane <m>y = c</m> gives
        <m>2x^2 + z^2 = c</m>: no trace for <m>c \lt 0</m>, a single point
        when <m>c = 0</m>, and ellipses that grow as <m>c</m> increases.  The
        planes <m>x = c</m> and <m>z = c</m> give the parabolas
        <m>y = z^2 + 2c^2</m> and <m>y = 2x^2 + c^2</m>, both opening in the
        positive <m>y</m> direction.  Ellipses on one side only together with
        parabolas: an elliptical paraboloid opening along the positive
        <m>y</m>-axis, graph E.
      </p>
      <p>
        For <m>y^2 = x^2 + 2z^2</m>, the plane <m>y = c</m> gives
        <m>x^2 + 2z^2 = c^2</m>: an ellipse for every <m>c \neq 0</m>, on
        both sides of the origin, shrinking to a single point when
        <m>c = 0</m>.  The plane <m>z = 0</m> gives <m>y^2 = x^2</m>, the two
        crossing lines <m>y = \pm x</m>.  Ellipses collapsing to a point,
        together with crossing lines: an elliptic cone along the
        <m>y</m>-axis, graph B.
      </p>
      <p>
        For <m>x^2 + 2z^2 = 1</m>, the variable <m>y</m> is missing, so the
        plane <m>y = c</m> gives the <em>same</em> ellipse
        <m>x^2 + 2z^2 = 1</m> for every value of <m>c</m>.  Identical
        elliptical slices at every station: a cylinder parallel to the
        <m>y</m>-axis, graph D.
      </p>
      <p>
        For <m>y = x^2 - z^2</m>, the plane <m>z = c</m> gives the parabola
        <m>y = x^2 - c^2</m> opening in the positive <m>y</m> direction,
        while the plane <m>x = c</m> gives the parabola <m>y = c^2 - z^2</m>
        opening in the negative <m>y</m> direction, and the plane
        <m>y = 0</m> gives the crossing lines <m>z = \pm x</m>.  Parabolas
        opening in opposite directions: a hyperbolic paraboloid, a saddle,
        graph C.
      </p>
      <p>
        Graphs A and F match none of the equations.  Slicing the ellipsoid
        in A gives bounded ellipses in every direction, which none of the
        equations produce.  Slicing the two bowls in F with planes
        <m>y = c</m> gives ellipses whose size grows like
        <m>\sqrt{|c|}</m>, unlike the straight-sided linear growth of the
        cone in B.
      </p>
    </solution>
    END SOLUTION-LOCKED -->
  </webwork>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rf-5">
<statement>
<p>
Identify and sketch the surface corresponding to the following
equation:
<md>
z^2 - y^2 - x^2 = 0.
</md>
</p>
</statement>
<!-- SOLUTION-LOCKED assignment="6" due="2026-10-28"
<solution>
<p>
We can rewrite the equation as <m>z^2 = x^2 + y^2</m>. Let us look at
its traces:
<ul>
<li>
<p>
<m>z = c</m>: the horizontal traces <m>x^2 + y^2 = c^2</m> are circles
of radius <m>|c|</m> (for <m>c \ne 0</m>).
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>x = 0</m>: <m>z = \pm y</m>, a pair of lines intersecting at the
origin.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>y = 0</m>: <m>z = \pm x</m>, a pair of lines intersecting at the
origin.
</p>
</li>
</ul>
The surface is a <term>double cone</term> with its vertex at the
origin, opening along the <m>z</m>-axis. See
<xref ref="fig-rf-cone"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-rf-cone">
